ProjectTitle:
The Newton Research Collaboration Programme aims to develop science and innovation partnerships between UK and developing countries to promote their economic development and social welfare. Awards funded through the scheme facilitate international exchanges lasting three months to a year between researchers in the UK and their counterparts in selected Newton Fund Partner Countries. Exchanges can be either one- or two-way in direction, and should form part of a collaborative research project, incorporating visits and dissemination activities. The overarching research project should constitute an internationally excellent research collaboration in any area of engineering. Applicants must demonstrate how the ultimate impacts of the research will benefit one or more developing countries in terms of social welfare improvements and economic development, as well as how the project will enhance research skills capacity in the Newton Fund partner country in identified areas of need.
(ii) To support outstanding research collaborations in engineering, between UK and a given partner country;
(iii) To build research capacity in developing countries in areas of identified national need;

ProjectDescription:
The project aims to increase access of Uzbekistan’s population to objective & inclusive media content and information by 
a) enhancing the  capacity of selected Uzbek media outlets to produce more objective and inclusive media content;
b)  enhancing the capacity of journalists, journalism students and the wider public on data journalism and identification of ‘fake news’.

The project aims to improve and simplify access to digital public services for all Uzbek citizens, in particular for vulnerable social groups, as well as to improve the effectiveness of public policy design processes to prevent corruption by: 
a) Improving the capacity of Uzbek Government to develop citizen-centric digital transformation strategies for public service delivery;
b) Enhancing operational capacity of Uzbek Government to develop effective regulatory impact assessment (RIA) and anticorruption screening.

Project aims to continue its support to the Government of Uzbekistan to deliver prioritised, co-ordinated and targeted governance reforms by: 
a) Providing MoJ with support on implementation of a selected number of capability review recommendations;
b) Strengthening the capacity of MoJ staff further;
c) Extending its support offer to the Presidential Administration.

The project aims to support the rehabilitation and reintegration services for children in closed educational institutions back into their families and communities by:
a) improving the quality of reintegration services provided to children leaving specialised closed institutions;
b) improving the rehabilitation services to children in these institutions through development and utilisation of individual reintegration and rehabilitation plans.

The Promoting HIV Self-testing for Young Men in Kenya project aims to close the HIV testing gap among men in Kenya by promoting HIV Self-Testing (HIVST).  Its objectives are:
• To innovate service delivery models linking public and private sectors through design of HIVST promotion tools targeting young people; 
• To increase uptake of HIVST through awareness raising on self-testing services for at-risk men ages 20-34;
• To create a sustainable supply of HIVST kits that are conveniently available and accessible for men in need of HIVST; and 
• To create an enabling environment for HIVST scale up and sustainability by adapting and running the intervention through existing health system and structures.

